course: world
lesson: 48
title: Time and history: the timeline and how people lived long ago
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: History is the story of how people lived in the past; we put events in order on a timeline, a line where each step stands for the same number of years, like the marks on a ruler.
explain: Years are grouped by hundreds into centuries: the 1st century is the years 1 to 100, the 20th century is 1901 to 2000, and we live in the 21st century, the years 2001 to 2100; so the year 1648 is in the 17th century: for a year that does not end in 00, the century is one more than the number of whole hundreds.
explain: The years before the year 1 are counted backwards and called BC, before Christ: an event in 3000 BC happened 3000 years before the year 1, about 5000 years ago.
explain: For most of history people hunted and gathered; about 10,000 years ago people began to farm, to keep animals and to live in villages; in Britain, farmers built the great stone circle of Stonehenge about 5,000 years ago, and in the Americas people learned to grow maize, a kind of corn, from a wild grass.
explain: Great inventions changed life: the wheel about 5,500 years ago, writing about 5,000 years ago in Mesopotamia, and printing with movable metal letters by Johannes Gutenberg around 1450, which made books cheap enough for many people to own.
fact: A timeline shows events in the order they happened, with equal steps for equal numbers of years.
fact: A century is a period of 100 years.
fact: We live in the 21st century, the years 2001 to 2100.
fact: The 20th century is the years 1901 to 2000.
fact: Years before the year 1 are marked BC and are counted backwards.
fact: About 10,000 years ago people began to farm and live in villages.
fact: Stonehenge in England was begun by early farmers about 5,000 years ago.
fact: The wheel was invented about 5,500 years ago.
fact: Writing was invented about 5,000 years ago in Mesopotamia, in the land between two rivers in the Middle East.
fact: Johannes Gutenberg began printing books with movable metal letters around 1450.
fact: Before farming, people lived by hunting animals and gathering wild plants.
fact: The United States declared its independence in 1776.
fact: Aboriginal Australians have lived in Australia for more than 50,000 years.
exercise: How many years are there in a century? | 100 | 10 | 1,000 | 50
exercise: In which century do we live now? | The 21st century | The 20th century | The 2nd century | The 22nd century
exercise: In which century is the year 1648? | The 17th century | The 16th century | The 18th century | The 6th century
exercise: In which century is the year 1991? | The 20th century | The 19th century | The 21st century | The 1st century
exercise: In which century is the year 1450? | The 15th century | The 14th century | The 16th century | The 4th century
exercise: What does a timeline show? | Events in the order they happened | The distances between towns | The highest mountains of the world | The days of a single week
exercise: How did people live before farming began? | By hunting animals and gathering wild plants | By buying food in markets | By growing wheat on large fields | By catching fish in big ships
exercise: About how long ago did people begin to farm? | About 10,000 years ago | About 100 years ago | About 1,000 years ago | About a million years ago
exercise: Who built Stonehenge? | Early farmers in Britain about 5,000 years ago | Knights of a medieval king about 800 years ago | Roman soldiers about 2,000 years ago | Hunters of the Ice Age 50,000 years ago
exercise: Where was writing first invented? | In Mesopotamia, in the Middle East | In London, on the Thames | In Australia, near the sea | In Antarctica, under the ice
exercise: What did Gutenberg's printing do? | It made books cheap enough for many people | It invented the first letters of the alphabet | It made the first paper from trees | It made the first wheels for carts
exercise: Which came first? | The wheel | Printing with metal letters | The telephone | The bicycle
exercise: Which event happened about 5,000 years ago, in 3000 BC? | People in Mesopotamia were already writing | Gutenberg printed his first book | The United States became independent | The Romans built roads across Britain
exercise: How many years passed from 3000 BC to the year 1? | 3,000 years | 2,000 years | 1,000 years | 5,000 years
exercise: In which century did the United States declare independence, in 1776? | The 18th century | The 17th century | The 19th century | The 7th century
test: Grandma was born in 1955 and her granddaughter in 2015. In which centuries were they born? | The 20th and the 21st | The 19th and the 20th | Both in the 20th | The 19th and the 21st
test: On a timeline, the wheel, writing and printing are marked. In which order do they stand, from oldest to newest? | Wheel, writing, printing | Writing, printing, wheel | Printing, wheel, writing | Wheel, printing, writing
test: Which people have the longest history in their land among these? | Aboriginal Australians, for more than 50,000 years | The first farmers of Britain, for 5,000 years | The people of the United States since 1776 | The printers who followed Gutenberg
test: A pot was made in 500 BC and a sword in the year 500. How many years apart were they made? | About 1,000 years | 0 years | 500 years | 2,000 years
test: Why was Gutenberg's printing so important? | Books could be made quickly, so many more people could read | It was the first time anyone wrote anything down | It let people read in the dark | It made the first maps of the world
test: Which is true about the 1st century? | It is the years 1 to 100 | It is the years 100 to 199 | It is the years 1000 to 1100 | It is the years before the year 1
test: Why did people who began to farm build villages, while hunters kept moving? | Farmers had to stay near their fields and animals | Farmers were afraid of the forest | Hunters did not know how to build houses | Villages were warmer only in summer
test: A writer of the 15th century wanted to send a letter to a friend. Which of these could he use? | Paper and ink | A telephone | A printed newspaper of 1950 | An email
test: The year 1240 belongs to which century? | The 13th century | The 12th century | The 14th century | The 2nd century
test: 1990 is that the 19th or 20th century | The 20th century | The 19th century | The 21st century | The 10th century
test: how'd people eat before there were farms | They hunted animals and gathered wild plants | They bought food in shops | They ate only fish from the sea | They did not eat until farms came
test: my classmate says 2005 is in the 20th century cos it starts with 20. true? | No, it is in the 21st century, the years 2001 to 2100 | Yes, the 20 shows the century | Yes, all years with 20 are the 20th century | No, it is in the 22nd century
test: Luke reckons 500 BC came after 200 BC. right? | No, BC years count backwards, so 500 BC came first | Yes, bigger numbers always come later | Yes, BC years count forwards | No, they happened in the same year
exam: State the number of years constituting a century. | One hundred | One thousand | Ten | Fifty
exam: Identify the years comprising the twentieth century. | 1901 to 2000 | 2001 to 2100 | 1800 to 1899 | 1900 to 1999
exam: State the approximate period since people began to farm and to dwell in villages. | Approximately ten thousand years | Approximately one thousand years | Approximately one million years | Approximately five hundred years
exam: Identify the year in which the United States declared its independence. | 1776 | 1492 | 1812 | 1865
